            <ArticleTitle>Vectorcardiographic changes during cardioangiography with iodixanol and ioxaglate.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>173-80</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To compare the electrophysiological effects of two contrast media (CM), the non-ionic dimer iodixanol and the ionic dimer ioxaglate using computerised dynamic vectorcardiography (VCG) during coronary angiography.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">The study was designed as a double-blind, three-period crossover, randomised comparison between iodixanol (320 mg I/ml) and ioxaglate (320 mg I/ml). Group 1 (HVV) received ioxaglate (H) in the first injection in the left coronary artery (LCA) and iodixanol (V) in the following injections. Group 2 (VHH) received iodixanol in the first injection in LCA and ioxaglate in the following injections. The first three injections in the LCA were subjected to electrocardiographic analysis.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">For five out of six VCG variables, there was a significant difference in response between iodixanol and ioxaglate. For these five variables, the deviations from baseline were greater in the ioxaglate than in the iodixanol group (P&lt;0.05). The most pronounced effects from ioxaglate were seen on the ST-segment and T-wave.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Iodixanol caused less pronounced electrophysiological changes than ioxaglate, especially during the repolarisation phase. Vectorcardiography is a sensitive and reproducible technique for detecting electrophysiological effects induced by CM.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
